+# ADR-009: Tool Execution Safety
+
+**Kind**: Implementation decision
+**Status**: Accepted
+**Deciders**: Flywheel Core Team
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+Every runtime tool explicitly declares whether it may overlap other calls, and serialization is reserved for provable local conflicts. One gate per model-turn attempt admits concurrency-safe calls up to a bounded cap; a file-mutating call that declares its targets reserves those paths and overlaps calls whose reservations do not overlap; the exclusive class is the fail-closed fallback for a call whose input demonstrates no usable classification. Later work never overtakes a queued call.
+
+Tool dispatch has two call origins: provider-originated calls and Code-originated nested calls. Both origins use one shared boundary and the same gates. Code is an ordinary tool with no outer gate permit; each nested call obtains its own admission before it runs. Chat and workflow runs share repeated-call detection.
+
+One lazy runtime-owned worker provides streamed-edit preview advice for every runner in that runtime generation. It receives coalesced bounded input, captures read-only file snapshots without retaining mutation authority, and retires state with the exact tool-input or attempt scope. Advice that is unavailable or arrives after its scope has retired never vetoes or delays execution. Final file-tool planning, publication, and settlement remain the sole authority.
+
+Every ordinary external process a tool starts is owned for its whole life. A launch goes through one boundary and runs inside a platform container Flywheel can address as a whole. On macOS and Linux, the process guardian adopts that container before the payload runs and records the lease where the next guardian finds it, so a hard kill of every owner at once leaves nothing running past the next start; on Windows, the container is created before the payload exists and the payload runs inside it from its first instruction. A launcher scope can require background scheduling, and every nested scope inherits that requirement. The requirement is established before the payload starts or the launch is refused. POSIX reads back the required nice value; Windows uses its background-priority process contract without a processor-affinity or reserved-core claim. The sole exception is the explicit, user-authorized persistent-process capability defined here. A planned replacement hands a running shell command from one build's owner to the next build's, and between the two owners the command's own supervisor holds it and enforces its deadline, so the command stays owned across the replacement.
+
+Flywheel has no general pre-invocation permission system. Model-originated workflow launches have one narrow domain admission: in Ask mode the workflow host verifies and consumes one matching `workflow_launch` decision before publication; in On mode no confirmation applies. This decision authorizes one workflow launch and provides no authority over any other tool or effect. The shared dispatcher also exposes one post-safety module gate: after validation, deterministic policy, capability admission, and concurrency admission, one typed gate may allow core execution, veto the call, or supply its complete execution function. The ADR-009 owner still runs and publishes that function under the same watchdog, process ownership, publication, and observation rules. Neither workflow confirmation nor the module gate provides blast-radius containment. Users who require containment supply a real environment boundary such as a VM, container, jail, low-privilege account, or disposable checkout.
+
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Unqualified parallel tool dispatch allowed mutations to interleave and let arbitrary shell effects race dedicated file tools. Per-file serialization could protect known paths but could not classify shell, network, or dynamically discovered effects.
+
+Separate foreground and background dispatch paths also risked inconsistent policy, while a hung implementation or repeated identical tool sequence could stall a turn indefinitely.
+
+A broad application approval prompt would not solve containment for an agent that can execute arbitrary shell commands. Workflow launch confirmation instead resolves one narrow product decision: whether a model may start one named background workflow. Ask mode verifies and consumes that decision at runtime; On mode launches without it. Neither mode changes process capabilities or containment.
+
+A tool that starts an external process starts something the operating system, not Flywheel, keeps alive. A payload can outlive its own exit through descendants that hold the terminal and the payload's streams, a signal aimed at one pid reaches one pid, and a host that is killed outright runs no cleanup at all. Bounding a tool call is therefore not the same as bounding the processes it started, and only a container plus an owner outside the host can bound both.
+
+---
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. Concurrency Safety Is Required Tool Metadata
+
+Every `RuntimeTool` declares `isConcurrencySafe` with no default.
+
+- Safe calls may overlap within the attempt's bounded concurrency budget.
+- An unsafe call whose tool declares `reservedPaths` and whose input names its mutation targets reserves the canonical realpaths of those targets. It may overlap safe calls and other path-scoped calls whose reservations do not overlap; overlapping targets (same realpath, or one inside the other's subtree) serialize. The file tools (`write`, `edit`, `apply_patch`) declare their targets; canonicalization uses the same physical identity as the file mutation queue, so "same file" means one thing in both layers.
+- Every other unsafe call (a call that failed input validation, a path-scoped call whose input yields no usable target, or a single-store local mutator) waits for active calls to drain, then runs exclusively.
+- Calls are served in arrival order; a queued call is never overtaken by later arrivals.
+
+Serialization exists only where a local conflict is provable and silent. File mutations serialize per target path: a same-file write race corrupts silently, and path reservations plus the file mutation queue prevent exactly that conflict and nothing more. Shell commands and network tools (web, MCP) are concurrency-safe: each shell call is a fresh isolated owned process with no shared session state, remote services own their own consistency, and the model batches only calls it judges independent, so residual conflicts fail loudly in the call's own output. The bounded cap is load control, never an independence claim. Per-resource locks remain useful defense in depth, but they cannot replace attempt-level admission because they do not understand arbitrary shell or network effects.
+
+### 2. File Mutations Retain Authority Through Settlement
+
+The state transport measures the complete encoded request before admission. Each durable payload crosses that boundary once. An indivisible request above the limit is a typed local persistence refusal, not a provider failure. Its notice states the byte size, the limit, and whether publication had started or settled.
+
+The shared publication boundary retains each accepted file's display evidence in session content-addressed storage. Settlement names that evidence independently of the final tool result. Tool results, checkpoints, and notices carry bounded metadata and references. File lists and patch bytes cross client and state boundaries in bounded windows. Missing, sensitive, or incomplete display evidence does not authorize Undo. Attribution continues to observe exact settled endpoint bytes rather than display evidence.
+
+The writer stages immutable chunks and manifest pages before settlement and proves their complete hash-and-length closure before accepting a reference. Evidence comes from admitted-before and verified-after bytes, including rebased Bash publications and operator Undo. Staging failure before publication changes no file. Storage failure after publication leaves the operation unresolved for conservative recovery; already-settled files retain their evidence. The retained policy keeps at most 1,000 rendered patch lines, exact omission metadata, strict UTF-8, and sensitive-hunk omission. Exact Undo checks the retained evidence and endpoint preconditions and refuses omitted, truncated, sensitive, corrupt, or unavailable evidence. Derived net patches are display only, not Undo authority.
+
+`edit` is the one file edit tool. It has two registrations, and the serving-model interaction profile selects one of them for each request: OpenAI GPT and peerAgent2 identities receive the peerAgent2 patch grammar presented as `apply_patch`, and every other model receives the string edit presented as `edit`. A presented tool declares its own wire shape; `apply_patch` carries the Lark grammar that OpenAI custom tools constrain generation with, and adapters build custom tools from that declaration rather than from the tool's name. The request surface records each presented name against its registration id and its tool name, and capability grants, bash policy, dispatch admission, and model-facing guidance read the tool name or the presented name through that map, so no consumer outside the profile spells a model next to a tool name. Both presentations plan differently and publish through one shared path that admits, prepares the durable operation, publishes under compare-and-swap, reads back, and settles. `read` and `search` return ordinary one-based decimal line numbers. Their output creates no editing protocol or cache authority. Each agent loop keeps one in-memory read ledger of what its reads returned (content hash and covered lines) and what its searches showed (covered lines only). A shell command that exits 0 and consists only of `cat`, `nl`, `bat`, `head`, `tail`, or `sed -n` reads of plain project file paths counts as a read of the lines it printed, unless output compression shortened them. A publication consults the ledger and attaches a warning when the target was never read in this loop, changed since it was read, or was read only in part that misses the replaced lines; a change with a later `file.changed` fact names that writer. The warning never blocks: Flywheel has no approval gates, and the compare-and-swap already refuses the one race that corrupts silently. A verified publication refreshes the ledger, so an edit that follows an edit is not stale. `write` refuses content whose every line carries a read line-number prefix. A successful update returns its unified diff hunks to the model, bounded to a model line limit while the UI keeps the longer rendering, so the model sees exactly what landed without a re-read; a creation returns only its path and line count because the model authored that content. `replace_all` publishes only when every occurrence matched exactly or through line-ending recovery; a normalizing tier that matched several occurrences refuses, because a fuzzy match must be reviewed one occurrence at a time.
+
+The string edit replaces one uniquely matched string in one existing strict UTF-8 file, or every match when explicitly requested. The patch edit accepts the bounded peerAgent2 V4A grammar for add, delete, update, and move. Deterministic formatting recovery may normalize line endings, proven read or search number prefixes, trailing whitespace, Unicode punctuation and spaces, or one uniform indentation shift. Similarity diagnostics never authorize publication. Input that is binary, malformed UTF-8, oversized, ambiguous, overlapping, empty where a match is required, or a no-op is refused before mutation.
+
+Parsing and planning are TypeScript in `src/infra/file-edit/`: the peerAgent2 V4A patch parser, the peerAgent2 seek tiers (exact, trailing whitespace, both margins, folded punctuation) that apply each chunk at the first match after the previous chunk, the string edit ladder, and the line-ending model. They receive text and caller-owned snapshots, enforce bounds, and return plans with replacement byte spans; they never receive a filesystem path and hold no write authority. A refusal names the path and the expected lines in plain words. The only native code in file editing is the Windows retained directory authority in `src/infra/descriptor-directory-win32-native.ts`, bound through `bun:ffi` to the kernel libraries every Windows process already runs on: handle-relative opens through `NtCreateFile`, snapshot reads, compare-then-rename publication, DACL evidence, and deletion. It is a platform primitive that only the win32 contained-directory backend loads, not an editing decision. TypeScript retains the physical parent authorities, canonical queue keys, exact byte and access snapshots, compare-and-swap preconditions, publication and deletion, verified readback, attribution, and durable settlement. Final targets that are symbolic links or reparse points are refused.
+
+Mutation preserves untouched bytes exactly. Text decoding is strict UTF-8, a UTF-8 BOM remains part of the byte preimage, and edits preserve existing BOM bytes. Existing LF, CRLF, and CR boundaries remain exact; a new logical line ending inherits the replaced boundary, then the nearest local style, then the file style. Ordinary POSIX mode and Windows DACL evidence is preserved without widening access, while privilege bits are not recreated.
+
+A multi-file file mutation fully preflights before publication, then publishes in its planned order under sorted canonical queue keys. A move publishes its destination before deleting its source, so every failure exposes an exact destination-then-source committed prefix. There is no cross-file rollback because rollback can overwrite intervening user work. Each successful publication is read back and verified before the next item begins. POSIX publication is descriptor-relative and conditionally renames only after the retained target still matches its admitted snapshot, but POSIX has no single kernel primitive that combines comparison and rename. Windows uses handle-relative native publication and exact DACL comparison rather than path re-resolution.
+
+One versioned `file.mutation` in-flight header names an immutable ordered plan in session content-addressed storage and records its cursor. The plan reference binds the cursor to one exact generation. The cursor identifies the only item that might have reached the filesystem; earlier items have terminal facts and later items are untouched. Each verified publication and its terminal facts advance the compact header atomically, and final settlement retires it. Recovery inspects bounded plan pages, reconciles only the current item, and retires the untouched suffix without publishing it. Recovery records `not_applied` only while the exact retained precondition still holds. A file larger than the retained precondition permits does not match that precondition. Matching post-bytes or absence do not prove that this publication produced them, so recovery settles the item as `indeterminate` without attributing a file change. Operation items record whether publication can create missing parents; an absent target with that evidence is also indeterminate because parent materialization might have started. An indeterminate settlement atomically retires the operation cell and produces one warning, so later starts owe no recovery for it. Terminal mutation facts remain durable history. Plans contain proof metadata and evidence references, never inline file or patch bytes.
+
+Completed `file.changed` facts retain endpoint access beside content and origin evidence. `beforeAccess` comes from the admitted snapshot and `afterAccess` from actual verified readback; `null` means known absence, not missing evidence. POSIX proofs retain the captured mode and Windows proofs retain the captured DACL bytes as base64. Access evidence has no second home in snapshot artifacts. Historical terminal facts remain readable without access fields and do not prove restoration permissions. Planned access, inherited defaults, and matching content hashes never substitute for captured endpoint access.
+
+For an update, overwrite, or move, access metadata is part of the requested mutation contract because the preexisting or source POSIX mode or Windows DACL must survive publication. A project file or directory that a tool creates belongs to the user, so on POSIX it gets the permissions the user's own tools would give it, 0666 or 0777 less the process umask, and the postcondition records that mode; only Flywheel's own state is created owner-only. A newly absent Windows target has no DACL to preserve: Windows chooses inherited access metadata during creation, and TypeScript cannot know it before publication without a second staging state. Only that postcondition records `access: null`, which places access outside the requested-effect proof rather than marking the mutation state unknown. The postcondition supports live verified settlement but does not authorize crash recovery attribution without publication-specific actor proof.
+
+### 3. Every Duration Has An Owner
+
+The shared dispatcher applies an inactivity watchdog to ordinary tools. Timeout returns a model-readable result and aborts the owned execution boundary rather than freezing the turn.
+
+An agent's wait on managed work has a separate soft timeout. The agent estimates the remaining duration of shell commands, subagents, and workflows and supplies that estimate to each wait call. The omitted-estimate fallback is four minutes. Completion or expiry consumes the wait and returns control to the agent without changing the work's lifetime. Expiry asks the agent to assess progress and either continue working or make another explicit wait call with a fresh estimate. The blocking input consumer owns one timer and input reservation per wait and retires both on wake, interruption, or run closure. No periodic assessment scheduler or command deadline carries this policy. Question and reply waits keep their existing end rules. Idle and interrupted sessions keep their own input ownership.
+
+A tool may opt out only by declaring `unboundedDuration` and identifying another lifecycle owner, such as a user-input wait or a command with its own timeout and cancellation contract. The flag transfers timeout ownership; it never authorizes an actually ownerless wait.
+
+The concrete cap and watchdog duration are tunable policy values in code, not architectural decisions.
+
+### 4. Repeated Tool Sequences Are Detected Consistently
+
+Chat and workflow workers use one doom-loop observer. Repeated identical call sequences produce a model-readable failure that requires a different approach rather than silently consuming the remaining budget.
+
+### 5. Code And Provider Calls Use The Same Gates
+
+Provider-originated tool calls and Code-originated nested calls pass through one shared admission, liveness, execution, publication, and observation boundary. Foreground, background, subagent, and workflow contexts do not create alternate execution policy. Async result delivery is not permission to bypass the boundary.
+
+Bash command policy awaits the shared parser loader at the point of use. Optional warmup is not a readiness prerequisite. If parser initialization or analysis fails, policy fails open for analysis-dependent checks regardless of the runner's file-tool grants. Independently recognizable command restrictions still apply. Tool availability and execution whitelists remain separate from command analysis. The Luna `exec` tool runs the same arbitrary commands, so it answers to the policy's tree-safety rules, forbidden Git operations and the worktree lifecycle gate, and to the read-only rule of section 7. It does not answer to the rules that redirect a command to a dedicated tool or limit `sleep`, because their advice names Bash affordances that `exec` does not have.
+
+Code is an ordinary `shell_exec` tool. Its outer invocation uses the declared no-permit admission variant, so it does not hold a permit while nested calls wait. Each nested call enters the normal safe, reserved-path, or exclusive admission path independently. Before an invocation runs, the executor's Code Mode process service admits it: at most four invocations run at once, fair across sessions. A call that waits there holds no nested batch and no Worker, and an abort while it waits removes it before either exists. That allowance belongs to Code Mode alone, so shell commands never consume it, and Code Mode never consumes theirs.
+
+The durable `source` input supplies one atomic JavaScript invocation body. Cumulative execution-output snapshots replace the live body, and the ordinary tool result content is preserved for every terminal outcome before status settlement. One prefix collector in the invocation's guest retains console output under Code's 50 KiB console budget and sends each newly retained line, or the drop marker, to the host, which mirrors it for live publication. Nested input previews have a separate 50 KiB budget that the host owns. The console snapshot is redacted as one stream before it is combined with separately redacted nested previews, so delimiters and truncation in one channel cannot change redaction in the other. These preview budgets do not truncate the returned value; the ordinary tool-output boundary retains oversized results in a breadcrumb.
+
+Each invocation runs in a new confined interpreter on a Worker of the service's pool, and a Worker runs one invocation at a time. The Worker receives the program and plain-data descriptors of the callable tools: names, paths, descriptions, and input schemas. Tool objects, functions, and dispatch never cross to it. The interpreter gives the program no ambient filesystem, network, process, import, timer, or evaluation capability. Every call the program makes returns to the host, which resolves the name against the exact callable map of that request and nothing else. A name outside that map, or an input that is not an object, is refused without dispatch, so Code-originated calls cannot expand the supplied tool map or bypass the shared boundary. The guest parses JSON-document results and redacts them after it parses them, so a JSON escape cannot hide a secret from the redactor. The host counts the aggregate result bytes, relayed image data included, and owns the one execution deadline, which starts when the host hands the program to the Worker. The Worker isolates the executor event loop; it is not a sandbox, and the heap budget is a sampled watchdog in the guest, not a hard cap.
+
+A Worker serves a later invocation only when nothing of the earlier one can reach it. The interpreter holds all program state, including the global scope, promises, and the console collector, inside the one execution, and it resolves names only against its own bindings, so a program can reach no Worker global, timer, or port. The guest keeps the invocation's pending bridge calls in one record that it drops at the reset, and call ids stay unique for the Worker's life, so a late reply can never resolve a call of a later invocation. The reset then collects the Worker's heap and takes the baseline that the next invocation's heap watchdog measures against, so the watchdog charges each program only its own growth. The host resets a Worker only after its program returned on its own. Every other ending terminates the Worker, and a terminated Worker is never leased again.
+
+On every exit the invocation's scope releases its resources in one order: it detaches the bridge; it returns the Worker, which the pool resets when the program returned on its own and otherwise terminates so guest work stops; it closes the nested batch, which aborts and joins every nested call and publishes each missing terminal; and then it flushes the logs once. The batch has this one close owner, so the tool result never settles before that join, whether the program finished, timed out, failed, or was aborted.
+
+Post-call hooks remain only where they have a concrete consumer such as execution notes. The only pre-effect module hook is the singleton tool-execution gate defined below; it is not approval.
+
+### 6. Module Gates Run Only After Safety Admission
+
+Provider-originated and Code-originated nested calls reach the same singleton tool-execution gate after input validation, deterministic execution policy, capability admission, and acquisition of the call's concurrency permit. A stale, disabled, policy-refused, invalid, or unadmitted call never reaches the gate.
+
+When no gate exists, dispatch calls the core tool implementation directly. A gate cannot approve a call or invoke the core implementation. It has exactly two answers: veto with a complete refusal reason, or replace with a complete execution function. A thrown gate error becomes a typed fail-closed refusal. Composition rejects a second gate for the seam at startup.
+
+The gate changes behavior, not ownership. Its decision input is deeply immutable normalized call data and validated input, with no runtime context or effect authority. The shared ADR-009 dispatcher invokes a replacement with only the call's abort signal and a bounded progress sink, under the same inactivity watchdog, durable publication, and lifecycle observation as core execution. The gate cannot publish directly, launch processes, create nested dispatches, hold or release permits, replace the dispatcher, or bypass earlier admission.
+
+### 7. Autonomy And Containment Are Separate
+
+Flywheel has no general `allow | ask | deny` policy language, per-tool approval system, or bypass mode. Its deterministic shell write-target rule guards against ordinary mistakes without restricting process capabilities. The module execution gate is an internal post-safety behavior seam, not user approval or additional authority. Ask-mode workflow confirmation is deterministic domain admission for the chat agent's file launch. The host requires an answered `workflow_launch` decision from the same session and conversation, bound to the same real path and definition hash. It claims the decision once before publication. Missing, rejected, mismatched, or consumed decisions refuse launch. A stored decision for a retired named workflow never authorizes a file. On mode exposes no confirmation field and rejects stale calls that carry one. This admission does not apply to skills, subagents, or other tools. There is no direct slash or protocol workflow launch. Changing the containment model requires a new ADR based on a different audience or deployment requirement.
+
+ADR-011's interactive global skill mutations apply without separate permission and disclose global scope in their tool results. Skill scope grants no process capability and is not a containment boundary.
+
+The bash policy interceptor may redirect commands toward better first-class tools for ergonomics. It is not a deny-list or security boundary and must not be described as one.
+
+Every Bash and exec call passes static write-target analysis before environment, script, spool, or process creation. This includes plain and `mutates` calls, foreground, background, and persistent execution. The rule admits writes and deletes inside canonical `projectRoot`, startup-owned `userDataRoot`, and all worktrees in the project's current Git registry, plus device sinks. The per-call working directory never adds a root. The registry is read from disk at each check without starting Git. A recognized target outside those roots, or one the analyzer cannot place, refuses the whole command. The result first says that the command was blocked because it is not a safe command. It names the target as written and directs the agent to the project or scratchpad; an unplaceable target also requires a literal path. It ends by telling the agent not to rephrase the command to reach the same target. One INFO diagnostic records the refusal kind, effect kind, tool, and correlation ids, with no command text or path. Allowed calls add no diagnostic.
+
+The analyzer follows statements, directory changes, assignments, wrappers, literal nested shell payloads, and substitutions in expanding heredocs. One shell-state owner tracks physical directories, logical directory names, shell variables, the directory stack, and paths whose shape an earlier effect can change. Assignments to `PWD` and `OLDPWD` change expansion, not the directory. Directory builtins update both. PowerShell directory commands and their aliases use the same transfer, without regard to case, with positional, `-Path`, or `-LiteralPath` operands. Ordinary `cd` resolves its operand logically, including `CDPATH`; target placement then follows physical path segments before applying `..`. A missing directory retains a possible failure path. AND/OR lists carry the matching successor state until control rejoins. Unknown directory state affects only directory-dependent targets. EXIT traps use their branch's final state; other signal traps use the merged states at which they can run, including paths on which the trap is later removed.
+
+The analyzer recognizes redirects and file operands of ordinary writers, removers, in-place editors, archive extraction, and writing find actions, including PowerShell forms. Writes follow final links; deletes preserve the final entry unless a trailing separator follows it. Globs use their static prefix and refuse parent traversal after a wildcard. A target through a path whose filesystem shape an earlier transfer or extraction can change is unplaceable. A literal `-` is a filename unless the command defines it as a stream marker. Unset variables without a literal fallback, unknown variables, dynamic targets, stdin-fed writer operands, and non-literal shell payloads are unplaceable. If analysis cannot load or complete, including exhaustion of its node, loop-convergence, wrapper, or payload depth budget, the command runs with the existing coalesced WARN; independent command restrictions still apply.
+
+This is a guard against mistakes, not containment or a command-name deny-list. Scripts, inline interpreter code, build tools, and dynamically constructed command names can write outside the roots. Reads and Git operations are not write effects for this rule; existing Git tree-safety restrictions remain separate. A plain Bash call's changes stay outside Flywheel's file-change records, edit cards, session change review, and attribution. A command declared with `mutates` puts its accepted file changes in those records (section 14).
+
+A persona is read-only when its own definition grants neither the file edit tool nor the write tool, as a persona that excludes the `file_edit` and `file_write` actions does; the explorer, the reviewer personas, and the advisor are read-only. That declaration is the mark. An agent of a workflow is read-only only when it runs as a read-only persona: every other agent of a workflow gets the same tools (ADR-006). The general clone is never read-only, and the implementer, which holds both tools, is not read-only. Dispatch derives the mark from the resolved persona and carries it to the child runner; the persona definition stores no extra field, so its identity hash stays the hash of what its file declares. The child's brief states the rule, while its shell tool shows the same schema and description as every other agent's, so that all agents share one prompt-cache prefix. The shell refuses `mutates` from a read-only agent. The same write-target analyzer serves every persona, but a read-only persona admits only its scratchpad and device sinks. It refuses recognized project, account, worktree, outside, and unplaceable targets. Temporary files outside the scratchpad are not allowed. The rule guards against ordinary mistakes and does not contain arbitrary programs.
+
+Project selection is not process containment either. Choosing a `projectRoot` decides where Flywheel-owned durable state and project context live; it grants and removes no operating-system capability from a tool that runs in the session's `executionCwd`. Static shell analysis refuses recognized outside writes, but arbitrary programs retain their environment's capabilities. A deployment that must restrict process capabilities supplies an external containment boundary.
+
+Recorded execution facts and diagnostics records provide accountability after execution. They do not prevent a process from exercising the capabilities its environment grants.
+
+An agent command inherits the whole environment of the process that runs it, with one change: its PATH starts with the session's scratch `bin` directory when the `bun` shim is installed, followed by the PATH of the person's login shell. A service that launchd or systemd starts has only the manager's minimal PATH, and a service that a terminal starts has that terminal's PATH, so the login shell's PATH is what makes the same command resolve the same binaries on every lane. The account service finds it after it listens and off the attach path: it runs `$SHELL` when `/etc/shells` lists it, else `/bin/sh`, as `-l -c "printf '\0'; env -0"` through the owned command authority at background priority, with HOME pinned to the real home and ZDOTDIR removed, for at most 15 seconds. It reads only PATH from the NUL-framed output, so text that startup files print before it cannot corrupt it. The owned container settles only when its process group is empty, so a background job that a startup file starts keeps the probe open until the bound, and the bound ends that job; a PATH entry that the shell already ended with a NUL is complete, so the account still uses it and records the timeout with it. Each project executor generation receives that PATH on its lifecycle channel, again each time that channel reattaches, and never runs the probe itself. While the probe has not finished, and after it fails, commands use the process's own PATH, and the account records the failure at WARN with the shell and the reason, never the PATH value. A failure is not kept: a project executor generation that opens while the PATH is unknown asks the account to probe again, and a repeat of the failure just recorded records at DEBUG. A PATH once found is kept for the life of the service. Windows runs no login shell and keeps its own PATH.
+
+### 8. Every External Process Uses One Of Two Launch Authorities
+
+An ordinary tool launch uses one of three entry points: `spawnOwned` for a process whose streams the caller reads, `runOwnedCommand` for a bounded command whose output is collected, and `openExternalUrl` for the one handoff to the user's desktop. Each takes a launcher scope, which is the whole ordinary launch capability a caller ever holds: it can start a process and it can close, and it never sees the guardian channel or the platform launch primitive.
+
+An ordinary launch is a container, not a pid. On macOS and Linux the container is a process group led by one per-command supervisor, which holds the launcher's own stream handles and passes them to the payload unchanged, so no payload byte travels through it. The supervisor exists because a payload cannot supervise its own container: it runs the escalation, and it exits with the payload's own status once the process group is empty. The supervisor sends all its diagnostic records through the return direction of control fd 3 on POSIX or its named pipe on Windows, never through payload stdout or stderr. The launcher drains that return channel independently of payload readers and relays each record once at its original level and service, with the supervisor's pid and the launch's context. A termination observer reads the original sender and caller into the existing container decision projection. Attached exit settlement waits for diagnostic EOF and relay before closing control; a Windows sender awaits native transport close before exiting. A raw launch error uses fd 2 only while the payload cannot have started. Payload bytes remain unchanged, including text that resembles a diagnostic record. POSIX persistent spools contain only payload output, and their retained control return channel uses the same relay and drain ordering. Handover retains its existing diagnostic suppression from acceptance through exit; its durable outcome records the decision. A Windows supervisor records one container decision per TERM or KILL request with membership-count evidence, then signals every other job member through a non-recording primitive.
+
+On macOS and Linux, ordinary ownership is established before the payload runs. The launcher spawns the guarded supervisor, the guardian adopts the process group by pid under a token, and only then does the launcher admit the payload through control fd 3. A background scope then establishes and reads back a `PRIO_PROCESS` nice value of at least 10 in the supervisor before it starts the payload. Failure to establish or verify that value is the same typed pre-payload refusal as failed ownership. EOF before admission refuses the launch. After admission, EOF or an explicit stop ends guarded work. Loss of the guardian also ends the ordinary process groups it held: the guarded supervisor watches the guardian's pid and identity, and the guardian ends every process group it still holds when its own channel closes, which is exactly what a SIGKILLed host produces. The supervisor ends its container only on proof that the guardian is gone: the pid is free, the kernel reports that the guardian exited, or the pid now holds a process with a different identity.
+
