+### 9. Settlement Is Ordered, And Emptiness Ends It
+
+One order settles an owned process, and every caller observes the same one:
+
+1. the caller closes what it owns first: its writes to the process, and any admission of new work into it;
+2. the launch's stop hold, when the launch declares one, defers the first signal; then the POSIX container supervisor sends TERM to its process group, waits one grace, then sends KILL to each proven member, and a stop or an end also sends each signal to each marked process outside the group; the Windows container uses its bounded tree termination;
+3. the platform container reaches its settlement observation boundary;
+4. bounded readers drain and the result is finalized;
+5. the ownership lease is released to the guardian; and
+6. the terminal result is published.
+
+TERM reaches every member of a POSIX container through one signal to its process group, so it never waits on a membership listing and a listing that does not answer never withholds it. The group includes the supervisor, which reads its own TERM as part of the escalation it is running and never as a request to stop. KILL goes to each proven member one at a time, because a group KILL would also end the supervisor, which must survive its escalation to report the payload's status and prove the group empty. A membership that does not answer sends no KILL on that pass and keeps the container owned for the next one.
+
+A launch can declare a stop hold: an absolute file path and a bound. While the file exists, no end of the launch signals its container, at most for the bound, and the grace between TERM and KILL starts after the hold. A payload declares one when a signal at the wrong moment damages state that outlives it. The peerAgent1 CLI holds a lock file while it refreshes its login's token, and a CLI that is signalled during the refresh can exit before the new token is saved, after which the next CLI clears the login. The hold travels with the launch to every process that can end the container: the launcher keeps it with the container it holds, the container's supervisor receives it in its argv, and the guardian receives it in the lease. `emptyContainers` is the one place that honors it, so an owner's stop, a scope or generation close, the supervisor's end when its controller or its guardian is gone, the launcher's emptying after a supervisor that died, and the guardian's generation close and sweep all wait for it, on every platform. The supervisor is the end that matters after a crash: it outlives the host, so the hold survives a host that is killed or that ends at its hard-exit bound. On POSIX it runs in a session of its own. On Windows the host's runtime ends the members of the job it keeps for its children when the host exits, so a launch that declares a hold starts its supervisor outside that job, as a launch that may be handed over does, and the supervisor ends its container itself when the host's channel ends. A hold delays only the containers that declared one, which escalate beside the others. A launcher scope's close deadline grows by the longest hold it and its nested scopes hold, so a held close is not reported unsettled while its hold still stands. The host's exit bound for its guardian grows the same way, by the longest hold that any lease asked of that guardian, so a disposal never kills the guardian while its sweep waits for a hold. The guardian accepts a hold of at most 60 seconds. One end does not wait: `flywheel kill`, which signals each process group it finds without asking.
+
+The exit of the process that leads the container does not end ownership. On macOS and Linux, process-group emptiness does: a descendant that remains in the group and keeps the payload's streams after the payload is gone is still Flywheel's to remove. A stop or an end also waits until each marked process outside the group is proven gone. On Windows, job-object membership does: the kernel reports exactly which processes the container still holds, so emptiness is read rather than inferred. The leader is the container's own supervisor, and a supervisor that is killed or crashes has ended nothing: its payload is still in the container, and the supervisor's guardian watch ended with it. So the launcher reports a supervisor's exit only after it has itself ended what the container still holds and proven the container empty. One absolute grace covers a whole close, so an owner holding many containers is bounded exactly like an owner holding one, and reaching that grace with containers still held is an explicit unsettled outcome, never a quiet success.
+
+Release before publication is the ordering that matters most. The guardian forgets a container before any caller can act on its exit, so a caller that reuses the pid or starts a replacement can never race the guardian's kill list. A release the guardian never answers is not a settlement either: the exit reports its ownership as unacknowledged, and the owner reads that rather than a report of a settlement nobody proved. A guardian whose process has exited is the one exception. Its kill list was a record in that process and ended with it, and every guarded container watches its guardian and ends itself once the guardian is gone, so a release or a generation close that finds the guardian's process exited reports settlement. A control channel that ends, or that sends a frame Flywheel does not define, while the guardian process lives proves nothing and ends no lease: every lease stays in force until the guardian exits. The host stops using that channel and ends that guardian the way its disposal does. It closes the guardian's stdin, which tells the guardian to end every container it still holds and exit, and it kills the guardian only when the guardian has not exited within the disposal bound. The proven exit then ends the host's side of that work as any guardian death does. A launch meanwhile waits for that ending and refuses with the channel's reason, so no new container comes to depend on a guardian that is being ended.
+
+### 10. Ownership Transfers Explicitly
+
+Launch capability is scoped. A scope closes with the owner that opened it, terminates every container it still holds, and refuses every later launch. A nested scope closes with its parent. Normal scheduling is represented by no priority policy. A scope can narrow itself to required background scheduling, and every nested scope inherits that requirement, so no descendant can widen background work back to normal priority.
+
+A close can end what the scope holds at once, for an owner whose processes hold nothing a stop would save. That close does not ask each container's supervisor and wait for it: it ends each container from this side with no grace, and a stop hold still delays it as it delays every other end. A container's supervisor runs at the scope's priority, and under load a background supervisor can wait a minute for the processor, so only an end from this side is prompt then. An end at once reaches the nested scopes and every container whose acquisition finishes after it, and it cuts short a termination that an earlier close asked for and that did not settle. The account closes a project executor that never reported ready this way (ADR-007).
+
+Closing reports what it proved. A scope that cannot settle a container keeps holding it and returns the reason, so a later close checks what remains. A runtime generation is replaced only after closure proves that the generation is empty.
+
+Bash foreground ownership is completion-first. A session holds every foreground command in its tool call from the moment the command runs, whatever its timeout, so Detach and a planned replacement's move to the background reach each one. A launch that still waits for shell capacity is not held yet: it has nothing to move, and a move never waits for capacity that only a running command can free. A foreground command remains in its tool call until the process settles, the user selects Detach for that exact tool call, a planned replacement moves it, the command reaches the foreground hold cap, or the turn aborts. A command whose explicit timeout is at or below the cap never reaches the cap: its deadline ends it in its tool call. Output activity does not change this order. Detach is an explicit ownership transfer, never an unconditional consequence of a tool return. One hidden, validated operator policy sets the cap for every foreground Bash call. The policy is not a model-selected timeout or a capability setting.
+
+Detach, a planned replacement's move, and cap expiry transfer the still-running command to the session registry. Registration with that registry is the ownership linearization point: before registration, turn abort terminates the process container; after registration, the session owns the process, its output readers, and its eventual result. Registration occurs only after the foreground race selects `user_detach`, `replacement`, or `runtime_cap`, so a process that settles first completes as a foreground call and never appears as managed background work. The terminal tool result records which transfer reason won; for `replacement` the model reads that the command moved because the runtime was replaced.
+
+The transcript exposes controls from the current owner. A held foreground Bash row offers Detach. After transfer is registered, that row offers Stop instead. Detach addresses one tool call and cannot release another held command. Stop addresses the corresponding session-registry task. These controls are sequential views of one ownership transition, not two concurrent ways to control the process.
+
+A request with `background: true` transfers to the session registry immediately after launch. Such a command is treated as a service for automatic completion: it does not hold the completion of a bounded run, and the close of its scope ends it like any other owned work. This classification does not restrict explicit waits or prevent a command from completing on its own. An agent of a workflow or a subagent does not hand off while a service that it started runs: the handoff tool refuses and names each running service, and the agent stops it first. Without an explicit wait, the handoff watchdog still asks for the handoff while only a service runs. A native `process` wait parks on live session-managed work, including commands launched with `background: true`, until completion or its `soft_timeout` expires. It parks on every process, so it ignores a process that the call names, and its result says so. A child that its parent or the runtime interrupted owes no result while it is interrupted, so no wait parks on it and no handoff waits for it, and the parent's own interrupt of a child ends the parent's wait on that child at once. A child that a person interrupted stays the parent's work: a wait parks on it, and a handoff waits for it. The `process` interrupt, resume, and stop are not concurrency-safe, and they run only after the assistant message that calls them commits, so a crash never leaves a control of a child without its call in the parent's history. Both model tool contracts expose that finite session wait; in the Luna contract it is the wait that names no process. The Luna named `process` wait instead names one command and returns after its `wait_sec`, so that variant never parks the turn. The same named input therefore parks under one contract and not under the other, so the runtime and crash recovery read the contract from the tool registration that each call records, never from the input alone. Shell-level `&`, `nohup`, and `disown` remain refused because they cannot create another lifetime beside the managed registry. Explicit background execution and foreground transfer use one host-owned `ShellRuntime` and one output owner before and after registration. On POSIX, the command's output log is readable only by the account. A command that settles in its tool call removes its script, and removes its log unless its result names the log for output too large to return whole. Bash and process adapters receive only its immutable client. While the command runs, in its foreground hold and after transfer, its output tail streams on the session registry's live channel, which outlives turns and runners; a running tail is shown, never recorded. The process and its readers settle, the guardian lease releases through the owned process exit, the registry records the settled execution output once, and then one live completion notification is published under the command's own key, `command:<processId>`.
+
+Ordinary shell launches share one process admission owner across the project runtime, including all hosted sessions and subagents. It admits at most ten active shell containers, matching one attempt's allowance without multiplying that allowance by the number of agents. Waiting launches are FIFO within each durable session, and admission rotates between sessions. Ephemeral work uses its artifact scope when it has no session identity. A launch holds capacity until its owned process exit observes container emptiness, including after explicit background launch, Detach, or automatic transfer, or until it hands its command to the next build. A command adopted from a previous build takes one slot at once, past the limit if need be, and never waits in the launch queue, because it already runs; a queued launch then waits until capacity frees. Launch failure releases capacity. Cancellation removes a queued launch without starting a process. Stop, Detach, and owner shutdown do not wait for launch capacity: a held command offers Detach while its launch still waits, and a Detach pressed then moves it to the background as it starts. This resource limit supplements attempt-level concurrency and does not change process ownership, persistent-process admission, or platform containment.
+
+Session close is the ordinary-work boundary. It stops new admission, aborts foreground and queued shell operations, hands every running managed command to the next build when the close is a planned replacement's, terminates every session-registry task that remains, drains their readers and settlement callbacks, suppresses later completion notifications, and then closes the host `ShellRuntime`. Runtime-generation and final host disposal use the same ordered close before releasing the guardian. A close cannot report settlement while the shell runtime still has active work.
+
+A planned replacement never waits for, ends, or reruns an ordinary shell command: it hands the running command to the next build (ADR-007). The command is what crosses, not its project and not its output. Its output is one log file in the session scratch directory, and its exit status belongs to its own supervisor, so neither moves. Every managed Bash command, foreground or background, launches able to be handed over. Shell payloads receive EOF stdin at launch because no shell tool can supply later input; an owner's exit cannot change that stream. Generic handover-capable processes retain their supervisor-held stdin contract. The cut first moves every command still in its foreground hold to the background with the release reason `replacement`, and it interrupts nothing until the registry tracks each moved command that runs or the command has ended, because an interrupt ends a command that is still held. A held command whose launch still waits for shell capacity is released without that wait: the interrupt cancels its launch before it runs, and the capacity it waits for may be held by the commands the replacement hands over. From that move on, a foreground command that the same session starts moves to the background as soon as it runs and never offers Detach. A host closes for the replacement in its executor's stop for a heard handoff, or when the replacement's pause ends a subagent child ahead of that stop. Every other close ends its commands as before, whether or not the executor heard a handoff: a person who deletes or closes a chat while the replacement drains, and a child or an agent of a workflow that settles on its own. A host that closes for the replacement hands over every running managed command before it terminates anything, in one order per command: it writes the command's handover row, sends the command's supervisor the handover word, and on the supervisor's acknowledgement releases the guardian lease and drops the registry entry with no kill and no not