+
+The handover row is in-flight state under the command's session (ADR-008). The successor's host for the same conversation adopts each row before its first model request: the supervisor takes the host's guardian and controller, the guardian adopts the container by token and pid, the executor's launcher scope holds the container as one it launched, and the registry tracks the command with its original start, its log tail, output reads, Stop, and the rest of its hard deadline, taking shell capacity at once. A command whose supervisor has already ended settles at once: with the exit status its supervisor recorded, as ended without a recorded exit status when its supervisor was killed while it held the command alone, or as ended with the previous runtime when it was never handed over. A chat publishes that completion as it publishes any completion, and an agent of a workflow and a child commit it into their conversation before the first request reads it. A row the host cannot adopt now is left and tried again at a growing interval, up to 30 seconds, until none is left or the host closes: its supervisor did not answer in time, another owner still held its channel, or it speaks no version of the adopt word this build sends, in which case the host offers no Stop and tracks nothing, and settles the row from its record once the supervisor has ended. A record of a version this build does not know leaves the row, and publishes nothing, for a build that reads it.
+
+Only a chat's host can be built without a run: an agent of a workflow and a child exist only while they run, and one a person paused runs again only when the person resumes it. So every row that no attached chat adopts, at the successor's continuation before any resume and at each later executor generation once it is ready, goes to the executor's keeper. The keeper holds each such conversation in one background execution owner, the owner a host holds its background work in, so it adopts, retries, stops, and hands over a command as a host does, with its guardian, Stop, and the rest of its hard deadline. Only what its adoption does to the row differs: it marks the row kept by compare-and-swap and leaves it standing while it holds the command. The kept row is the one durable fact that the command runs for its session and no host of the session holds it, and every status of the session, the one the account reads from history included, shows each kept row's command with its Stop. A row that nothing holds shows nothing, so a command whose supervisor speaks another version of the adopt word, or one an adoption has not reached yet, offers no Stop that nothing could answer.
+
+The keeper publishes the command's bounded live tail from the same output log and includes it in transcript snapshots. The account routes reads for kept commands to their executor without inventing an attached runner. A host that later holds the conversation takes the command and its current tail from the keeper before its first request, with its shell capacity, and retires the row, so its turn parks on the command. The receiving host accepts the tail before the keeper releases its copy. A command that ends while kept records its settled tail for its tool call, then replaces the displayed body and removes its live tail in one output update. The row remains visible while that final body becomes available. The command retires its row with its completion held for its conversation; the keeper writes no conversation, because a host may hold it at any time. Deleting the session ends what the keeper holds for it, and a kept command keeps its project open while it runs. The keeper closes once every host of its executor has closed: a planned replacement's stop hands its commands over again, and any other stop ends each one, whose end retires its row with its completion as any end does.
+
+Whoever adopts or settles a command retires its row by compare-and-swap on the row as read, so of two consumers exactly one wins. The consumer that retires the row of a command that ended holds the command's completion for its conversation in the same in-flight step, so exactly one consumer publishes each completion and a consumer that loses the row publishes nothing: the predecessor for a command whose supervisor did not take the handover, and the successor, a host or its keeper, for every other. Every publication is keyed by the command, `command:<processId>`. A delivery or a commit of a keyed notification that its conversation already committed adds nothing, retires any hold left under the key, and wakes no turn, and a save point names each notification's entry by its key. A held completion is the one path a background outcome takes into a conversation with no running turn: the conversation's next run commits it before it reads its context, which is the successor's automatic continuation for interrupted work and the person's next message for a session whose turn had finished. The consumer that settles an ended command removes its outcome record and, on POSIX, the socket a killed supervisor left; a host that adopted a command removes the record once the command settles, and the keeper once the row it retires holds the completion. `persist_after_exit` processes are untouched and are surrendered at service exit as always.
+
+An explicit timeout is bounded to 3600 seconds and remains the command's hard deadline after registry transfer and across a handover. The deadline starts when the process launches, terminates the owned container at expiry, and does not publish until process settlement. Without an explicit timeout, transfer does not add a deadline. A turn abort before transfer terminates the command and reports interruption. Session, runtime-generation, guardian, and host closure still terminate transferred ordinary work once, except a command that a planned replacement handed over.
+The browser handoff is the one transfer outside Flywheel. `open`, `xdg-open`, and `cmd /c start` hand a URL to a desktop service and exit, so the opener is owned as a bounded process and the browser it starts belongs to the desktop session. Success is reported only after the opener's container settles. A value that is not plain http(s) never reaches the launch.
+
+The handed-over lifetime is the supervisor's released lifetime, entered mid-run by one word beside `go` and `stop`, and only on a launch that asked for it. A supervisor accepts the word only from its current controller and only while no stop is pending. Before it acknowledges, it stops its guardian watch, serves one channel named by the ownership token (a Unix socket in the account's own service directory on POSIX, named by a digest of the token no longer than the account socket beside it, and a named pipe on Windows), declares its build live in the account until it exits (ADR-014), arms the command's recorded hard deadline, and silences every diagnostic it would write to its inherited streams, which belong to a launcher that may be gone. Only then does it write its outcome record, a versioned file beside the command's log that names the supervisor's own container identity where its platform reads one, and that record is the acknowledgement. A Windows background script writes a terminating error to its log too, so nothing of a handed-over command reaches a stream of a runtime that is gone. From then on the end of the predecessor's channel releases the command instead of ending it, the supervisor ends its container itself at the deadline, it records every payload outcome in that file before its channel closes, and it accepts one controller at a time on its channel. An adopting owner connects first, which proves the supervisor alive, so a reused pid is never leased. It sends the adopt word with its guardian's pid and identity, and its guardian adopts the container by token and pid only once the supervisor has answered, so an adoption the supervisor never answered leaves that guardian untouched. An adoption the supervisor answers after the bound, or whose lease the guardian refuses or does not answer in time, is given back to the supervisor with the handover word, and a lease that did not answer in time is compensated by a release. The supervisor then watches that guardian, reads the end of the new owner's channel as the end of the command, as a guarded supervisor does, and leaves the rest of the deadline to that owner, which can hand the command over again at a later replacement. The new owner keeps the channel open to see the command's end, so its stop word is the end a launcher's stop and close make: the supervisor retries until its container is empty. A channel that nothing serves means the supervisor is gone or cannot be reached, and the owner ends what it left before it reports the command ended, so no payload outlives the owner that reports its end. On POSIX no process can take the supervisor's pid while its group has a member: a group whose leader pid is free holds only what the supervisor left, and it is ended; a pid that now leads another group, or no group, proves the supervisor's group empty; and a pid that leads a group with the identity the supervisor recorded is the supervisor still running, so its row stays. On Windows a job that no longer opens ended its members with its last handle. On POSIX the supervisor already leads its own session, so the old launcher's exit and its service manager's restart reach nothing of it. On Windows a supervisor that may be handed over is launched outside the job the launcher's runtime keeps for its children, which ends its members when the launcher exits, and the supervisor's own handle on its container job, which it holds for its whole life, carries the container while the launcher and its guardian close theirs. The handover row, the channel's words, the outcome record, and a mutating command's copy record each carry a version from their first release. A supervisor answers an adopt word of a version it does not speak by naming the versions it does, and a reader that meets a row or a record of a version it does not know reports it and leaves the row or file exactly as it is. Stop, the agent's own kill, and the hard deadline end an adopted command as they end one its owner launched, a crash of the adopting owner ends it through that owner's guardian, and `flywheel kill` ends a handed-over supervisor and its command as it ends every other Flywheel process.
+
+### 11. Persistent Processes Require Explicit Authorization At Every Boundary
+
+Persistent processes are OFF by default. The effective host policy is enabled when either the persisted root configuration `allow_persistent_processes` is true or the current CLI invocation supplies `--allow-persistent-processes`. A launch also requires per-call opt-in. For Bash and the Luna `exec` tool, enabled policy adds `persist_after_exit` to the request schema and a caller must send `background=true` with `persist_after_exit=true`; disabled policy omits that field and rejects a stale explicit request. No ordinary background request, foreground cap, Detach action, planned replacement's handover, session close, or runtime close implicitly admits persistence.
+
+Persistent launch accepts no terminal stdin. It redirects stdout and stderr to mode-0600 spool files in a mode-0700 directory of their own. Every such directory sits in one spool root per user in the temporary directory, `flywheel-spools-<uid>` (`flywheel-spools` on Windows, whose temporary directory belongs to one user), which is used only when it is a real directory that the user owns and nobody else can enter, because a shared `/tmp` lets another user create the name first. The persistent authority alone owns those paths and their lifetime. On POSIX, the persistent launch authority starts the shared command supervisor as a detached process-group leader with the spool descriptors and control fd 3, then admits the payload through that exact channel. EOF before admission refuses the launch. After admission, EOF releases persistent work without terminating it, while an explicit stop requests the shared TERM, grace, KILL, and group-empty settlement. The persistent lifetime has no guardian watch. One launch shape is self-managed: Flywheel's own project runtime (ADR-007) starts detached with the retained spools and no command supervisor in front of it, because it supervises its own process tree through its own guardian and ends only through its own ordered shutdown, so no second process start precedes it and a stop signals the runtime itself. The process-lifetime manager lives in the CLI host and retains live records across session and runtime-generation closure. Each runtime generation receives only an ephemeral Worker bridge client; bridge closure does not change host ownership.
+
+A spool lives exactly as long as a process can use it. The launcher holds it while it can still read the output, until it releases the process, and the writer holds it until the writer exits. As soon as the writer starts, and before a supervised payload is admitted, the launch records both holders in the spool directory, each by its pid and by the start identity the kernel reports for it: the unique id on macOS, the start time on Linux, and the creation time on Windows. A pid alone proves nothing, because the system gives a free pid to the next process. A release removes the launcher's hold. A released spool whose writer has already exited is removed at once. The exit of a writer that outlives its release is seen by one of two owners. The launcher, while it still runs, reaps its own child and removes the spool. Otherwise the next persistent launch by the same user sweeps the spool root in the background and removes each spool whose holders are all proven gone. The sweep reads only the root, never the whole temporary directory, which can hold hundreds of thousands of entries. A holder is proven gone only when its pid holds no process, or holds a process with another start identity. On POSIX the writer leads its own process group, and it is proven gone only when that group also runs nothing, because every process in it inherited the spool's descriptors. A holder the kernel cannot answer for, a spool without a holder record, and a record in a shape the build cannot read all keep the spool. The spool's lifetime is the only persistent decision that reads a POSIX identity; launch, release, and stop never do.
+
+While the CLI host remains alive, the process tool can list persistent records, report live status, read retained stdout and stderr, and stop a captured process container. A POSIX stop writes to the retained control channel and awaits that exact supervisor's bounded settlement. The persistent supervisor carries a mark of its own, so its stop reaches every process the persistent command started, and never the container of the launch that started it. If the supervisor does not exit within that bound, the stop reports `unsettled`, retains the channel, and a later stop can request another bounded settlement attempt. A Windows stop checks its captured identity and applies the bounded tree contract. On POSIX, a naturally exited payload causes the supervisor to terminate any remaining in-group descendants, prove group emptiness, and exit with the payload status. At final host release, the manager closes POSIX control authority or releases the Windows record without terminating persistent work, including after an unsettled POSIX stop. Released or exited work has no Flywheel management surface: Flywheel cannot list, read, stop, or rediscover the process after the record leaves the manager, and it removes the spool only under the lifetime rule above.
+
+Persistent processes are supported on macOS, Linux, and Windows. On macOS and Linux, the detached persistent supervisor and its retained control channel provide the launch and stop contract without PID identity probing or host-side process-group signaling. On Windows, a persistent process is the one container that cannot be a job object, because a job is reached through a handle and a handle dies with the process holding it; the pid plus creation identity and bounded tree contract in the platform-strength section provide its launch and stop behavior. Unsupported platforms refuse before spool creation or launch. This exception does not change ordinary owned behavior: ordinary processes remain guardian-adopted where the platform supports that contract, session or generation bounded, and terminated through the existing settlement path.
+
+### 12. Platform Strength Is Stated, Never Assumed
+
+On macOS and Linux the guarantee is process-group ownership with the container mark: one signal reaches every member, the kernel's own group membership (`/proc` on Linux, libproc on macOS) proves group emptiness, and a group leader's pid names the container for exactly as long as the group exists. A stop, an end, and a guardian's sweep also reach each process that left the group and carries the mark, under the rule in section 8. A process that clears its environment, and a macOS platform program whose parent exited before the command ended, leave this boundary. Hostile escape containment requires an external sandbox.
+
+On Windows the guarantee is a job object, which is the platform's process group. A job is created before the payload exists, every process a member starts joins it automatically, the kernel reports its exact membership, and one call ends the whole set. Every job enables `KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`. A background job also enables `JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_PRIORITY_CLASS` with `BELOW_NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS`; creation reads both policies back through the pointer-width-correct native layout before assignment, and any establishment or readback failure refuses the launch. Ending a background job first raises its members to normal priority: a process ends only when its threads run, and under load a below-normal thread waits seconds for the processor, so a job ended at background priority would hold its owner's settlement that long. A member may not leave: breakaway is not permitted on these containers, so a process that asks to escape is refused by the kernel rather than lost by an owner. One container is made otherwise: a project executor's container lets the processes its members start stay out of it silently, but only from the moment the executor runs in it. The executor's supervisor starts the executor as its ordinary child, so the executor is a member of the container, and of the job the supervisor's runtime keeps for its children, before its first instruction runs; the supervisor then sets the limit, because a container that already lets children leave would not take the executor either, and it ends the executor if the limit cannot be set. No moment exists where the executor runs with no owner, so a supervisor that dies under any load takes the executor with it. So only a command's own container decides its end, as each POSIX launch leads its own session, and the executor's exit ends no command that a planned replacement handed over. Every process the executor starts leaves the executor's container this way, not only its commands, and each keeps an owner of its own: a command's supervisor is its own container, which the executor's guardian lease and launcher scope end at a stop and the guardian's sweep ends after a crash; the executor's guardian outlives the executor only to sweep those leases, and exits when its channel ends; the short kernel and access-control launches the executor awaits end before it goes on; and a persistent process is persistent by design. Ownership is the handle, and `KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE` makes Windows end the container when the last handle to it closes, so a host and a guardian that both die take the container with them without either one running code. Hostile escape containment requires an external sandbox on every platform.
+
+Background priority lowers scheduler preference only. Flywheel makes no CPU affinity, CPU reservation, quota, or exclusive-capacity claim. Control and state processes remain at normal priority because their launcher scopes omit the background policy.
+
+The container is named, and its name is the launch's ownership token. That is what lets the guardian own a container it never launched: it opens the container by the name it was told, with no handle to inherit and no pid to trust. A token names one launch, so a container's name is unique for all time and the recycled-identity question does not arise on Windows at all.
+
+One difference from POSIX remains, and it is about admission rather than containment. Bun exposes no pre-exec hold on Windows, so the payload starts before the guardian has been asked to own its container; it starts inside that container, so a refused ownership ends the payload and everything it started rather than leaving anything running. A persistent process is the exception in the other direction: it must outlive this process, so it cannot be held by a handle, and its identity is checked as pid plus OS-reported creation time before a stop acts on its tree. The process table names a parent by pid only, and a pid is recycled, so the tree admits a child only when its parent is an admitted member and the child was created no earlier than that parent and no later than the snapshot of the table. A process that names a recycled pid as its parent, and a pid reused after the snapshot, therefore never join. A candidate whose creation time cannot be read leaves the tree unproven: nothing is ended, and the tree stays owned, after the root exits too. The tree keeps the candidate until a query proves it gone, which is no process at its pid or a creation time outside the window a descendant's falls in, or proves it a descendant, which then joins as a member. Each member is ended with `TerminateProcess` through a handle on which its creation time was checked, so a pid recycled between the check and the kill is never the process that is ended, and no program runs to end the tree. A persistent stop that cannot establish the required outcome within its bound returns an explicit `unsettled` result rather than quiet success.
+
+### 13. Package Files Are Read Under Declaration And Containment
+
+The `skill` tool is read-only. It returns one package's instructions and returns one file that package declares; the catalog of installed packages is advertised in the system prompt. It runs nothing and changes no state, so it is concurrency-safe and overlaps other calls freely.
+
+An installed package is third-party content, and its tree can change between discovery and read. Readable files are therefore declared once when the package loads, from a depth-bounded walk that admits contained regular files only. A read must name a declared path, must still resolve inside the package after symlink resolution, and must still be a regular file. One call returns at most one bounded window, measured against the opened file rather than the size recorded at discovery, so no package file can pull an unbounded amount of text into context. A resource larger than one window is paged by byte offset through the same containment checks, never truncated to a half procedure and never handed to a general-purpose file read. A window decodes strictly: bytes that are not UTF-8, and an offset that falls inside a character, are refused rather than replaced or realigned, because a lossy window shifts every following page. Every refusal is a model-readable result, not an exception.
+
+### 14. Declared Bash Mutations Publish Through The File Edit Path
+
+A Bash command without `mutates` writes in place, and its changes stay outside the file-change records. A Bash command declared with `mutates` runs in a private copy taken when it starts, so another agent's concurrent writes never appear in it or get attributed to it. It captures only changes to file contents. Its project root and effective working directory resolve to physical paths before containment and copy placement, so directory links and case aliases keep the same authority. When its working directory is inside the project, the copy covers the canonical project root. Otherwise, the copy covers the Git work tree that contains its working directory, including a linked worktree with its own Git directory and index. A working directory outside both the project and every Git work tree is refused before the command runs. The selected root owns the copy, its OS guard, file publication, and the record used after a build handover.
+
+Agents run Git commands as separate calls without `mutates` and never chain them onto a mutating command. A PATH-first Git wrapper refuses repository-write modes before invoking Git and tells the agent to rerun the Git command as a separate call without `mutates`. Read-only Git commands, including scripts and build tools that only read Git, remain allowed. Agents set `mutates: true` only when a command exists to change the project files they are working on, so that the change is recorded and reviewed like a file tool change. Examples are heredoc and `cat >` writes, `sed -i`, scripts that rewrite files, formatters, generators, and plain `mv` and `rm`. Agents leave it unset when the writes are side effects of running something: dependency or binary installs, builds, tests, caches, and servers. They name the target tree with `cwd`, not an absolute `cd` in the command. They create scripts with the write tool and use Bash only to run them. The declaration is the first input field, so the row reads Edit while the command arrives. Once files land, added files use the write content presentation and its syntax palette; modified and removed files use edit diffs. The row reads Write when every landed file was added, otherwise Edit. Every client derives these choices from the shared file-change model.
+
+Wholly ignored directories are shared with the selected tree instead of copied. The running session's entire folder, `.flywheel/sessions/<id>/`, stays shared: writes land directly and are reported as reviewed file changes by comparison with a before image inside the copy home. The image excludes `owner.pid` at the session folder root. At the scratch root only, it excludes `mutations/`, runtime background command files named `flywheel-agent-bg-*`, and foreground output spools named `flywheel-foreground-*`. Other entries are cloned as whole files or directories; scratch itself is cloned by its eligible root entries. A concurrent write to the same session folder during the command is reported as the command's. The runtime derives the session folder from the known copy home under `<sessionDir>/scratch/mutations/`, verifies the `scratch` and `mutations` names, and never follows a command-writable link to find the root. In-memory fingerprints of the image detect removed, replaced, or changed files and report that session-folder changes could not be verified. Only regular image files are read; image links are never followed. A later build has no in-memory fingerprints and trusts the retained regular image files without proving their integrity. Copies without an image observe no session-folder changes. Session-folder observation runs on every settlement, including interruption and repository refusal, before the image is discarded. It records evidence only and never publishes session-folder writes or adds them to Session Changes. Other session folders and runtime stores stay outside this observation. The scanning Worker transfers both byte endpoints and computes binary identities so the runtime does not copy or hash large binary files. The copy is a copy-on-write clone where the filesystem supports one and a full copy where it does not, as on most Windows volumes. A project is outside Git only when Git says so and no `.git` entry stands at or above it. A Git that times out, is ended, or refuses a repository it found fails the copy, because a copy that shares no ignored directory clones every dependency tree and build output. Capture fails open after root selection: a command whose copy cannot be taken runs in place, its result says that its changes were not recorded as mutations, and one `agent.bash.mutation.uncaptured` record beside `agent.bash.mutation.applied` gives the capture failure rate. Git-write refusal still applies when capture is unavailable. A command whose Git wrapper cannot be prepared is refused before launch.
+
+Each command has a private Git directory in its copy home so permitted read-only Git sees the repository's history, refs, HEAD, index, configuration, excludes, and stash history. Git initializes that directory with the repository's object format and ref backend, including SHA-256 and reftable. Git alternates read the real common object store; no shared object directory is symlinked into the copy. A `.git` file at the copy tree root names the private directory. Git discovers the repository from its current directory, including after a `cd` into another tree. A project below the Git top level is laid out at its prefix, and index entries outside the project are marked skip-worktree. The private config preserves the repository format, common config, worktree config only when `extensions.worktreeConfig` enables it, and effective excludes, then fixes the private work tree and excludes file and disables automatic garbage collection. A split index brings its shared index files and becomes a standalone private index. Read execution suppresses optional locks, fsmonitor, and configured textconv cache writes. After setup completes, one semantic repository baseline belongs to the file-only execution policy. The baseline supports the landing backstop, not Git publication.
+
+On macOS, Seatbelt refuses writes to the real selected tree, all registered work trees of the same repository, and the real Git directories. Literal ancestor rules also refuse removal or renaming of directory nodes that contain protected roots, without closing their writable contents. On Linux, bubblewrap mounts those paths read-only. Shared entries of the selected tree stay writable, but never reopen its Git directory. The session scratch directory and copy home stay writable even when they lie inside a guarded tree. Protected trees and Git directories inside these writable allowances stay protected, including the shared entries' copied aliases on Linux. A refused write names its target tree in the result and tells the agent to select it with `cwd`, including when the error names both a private source and a protected destination. A mixed-path error with a relative destination names the protected roots when no exact root can be identified. Other folders are not guarded. Windows runs the copy unguarded by the OS mutation guard, with a PATH-first native Git wrapper, the same private Git read view, and file-only landing. A macOS or Linux host whose guard launcher is unavailable runs the command in place through the uncaptured path and reports that capture was unavailable. Git-write refusal still applies.
+
+When the command exits, foreground, auto-yielded, or background, landing checks its repository baseline before publishing any project file. The check compares HEAD and index entries by path, mode, object id, and stage. For the files backend, it compares all refs including symbolic targets and the ordered stash stack. For reftable, it hashes the active table list and every listed table in order, without reading objects or migrating formats. This stack includes dangling symbolic refs and all reflogs. The private copy seeds its reftable stack directly from these complete metadata bytes. Read-only Git commands do not change the stack; any ref or reflog write changes it. A missing table or a list that changes during inspection refuses the comparison. The check also rejects unexpected root or nested Git registrations. A detected change or an unavailable comparison applies no project file changes, preserves the copy and its record, and names the copy in a bounded recovery report. Session-folder writes already landed and remain reviewable in the command's evidence. Raw index stat refreshes do not count as semantic changes. Git objects, the index, HEAD, refs, stash, configuration, remotes, hooks, and registrations never land from the copy. The wrapper and backstop form a cooperative command boundary, not hostile-process containment. Absolute executables, replaced search paths, and in-process libraries can bypass the wrapper. Worktree-only bypass changes can land when the repository baseline remains unchanged. The final-state comparison does not prove which program wrote a file. The files-backend comparison does not detect a change that was reversed before landing; the reftable comparison rejects ref or reflog writes even when their final values match the baseline.
+
+Each added, changed, or deleted regular project file publishes through the shared file edit publisher as a mutation of its tool call. A base cloned from the copy pins the bytes the command started with. A file changed in the real tree meanwhile is rebased with `git merge-file` against that base and the command's result. Independent hunks merge quietly; overlapping hunks publish conflict markers named in the result and card. Concurrent creations merge against an empty base. A concurrent deletion or a binary conflict keeps the real file and reports the command's change as not applied. Publication retries a racing file at most three times. Other files still land. The diff starts from the real file's current content, so attribution holds only the command's changes.
+
+An interrupted current-policy command publishes no project file changes. Its session-folder writes remain in place and are reported as mutation evidence. `mutates` cannot be combined with `persist_after_exit`, because nothing would remain to land the copy. After private Git seeding and project-prefix setup complete, taking the copy finalizes its repository-state baseline and writes its versioned record before the command starts. The record binds the file-only execution policy, semantic baseline, real and private Git locations used by reads and guards, file and link observations, roots, shared entries, and guard. The file base pins the contents used by later publication and rebase. A reader converts supported older records without granting them the current execution policy. An older Git-backed copy publishes neither project files nor Git state, stays available for recovery, and reports that its changes cannot be proven to be only the command's own file edits. A legacy record that proves the command ran outside Git can use file-only landing. Git changes an earlier build already made outside its copy are not reversed. An unreadable record is reported and preserved. Kept copies remain subject to normal scratch retention. A discarded copy loses its record first, so a copy whose files cannot be removed at once is removed by the idle scratch sweep within an hour (ADR-008).
+
+A planned replacement hands the command and its copy home to the next build (section 10). The launching build then neither lands nor deletes it. The adopting host, executor keeper, or supervisor-record consumer uses the same landing and original tool identity, without the in-memory read ledger or Git AI attribution identity. Two consumers in one process share one landing, and the consumer that retires the row publishes the completion and recorded tail once. A runtime death without a recorded command exit is an interrupt. An unreadable or missing record lands nothing, keeps the copy, and reports its path and reason once. The lineage-only command rules do not apply to declared mutations because their accepted file writes are recorded. A command without `mutates`, including a Git repository write, runs in place with no mutation diff and no file-change attribution.
+
+Bun retains a native Mach right for each Worker lifetime on macOS, so the sole Worker constructor authority and CI ownership gate are safety contracts, not only retry policy. CI resolves Worker and MessageChannel aliases, fixes every role allocator to one reviewed owner site, and fixes the reviewed channel counts so neither a fresh counter nor an extra native port can enter through an allowlisted function.
+
+### 15. Streamed-Edit Validation Is Advisory
+
+One optional validation service belongs to the runtime generation and is shared by hosted, child, chat, and workflow runners. It starts its sole worker lazily when the first active streamed edit supplies input. The runtime does not create one worker per runner, turn, or tool call.
+
+The service coalesces incremental suffixes per tool-input identity and sends only one batch at a time across all active inputs. One global input-byte ceiling covers every accepted suffix until its input is retired, whether queued, in flight, or already consumed by the worker. An input that would exceed it is retired instead of creating another queue or worker. The service assigns each input a unique internal handle, so equal provider IDs and local attempt counters from separate runners cannot share parser state, snapshots, or replies. Tool settlement retires that input, and attempt reset retires only inputs owned by that turn. Replies are accepted only for the current in-flight handle, identity, and sequence, so retired or superseded work cannot alter another input or the transcript.
+
+The worker may capture strict text snapshots only through a retained file authority that it closes immediately after each read. A separate worker-owned ceiling bounds aggregate retained snapshots across live inputs using the existing file-edit output limit. An input whose snapshot cannot fit becomes unavailable and releases its retained state without affecting other inputs. The worker never retains a path authority, publishes bytes, acquires a mutation permit, or contributes a precondition to final execution. Binary or otherwise unreadable snapshots, worker startup or runtime failure, disposal, late replies, and retired input make preview validation unavailable or irrelevant. These outcomes clear or omit advice and never refuse, delay, or change the tool call.
+
+The validator uses the same bounded patch and string-edit planners that establish syntax and applicability, but only to produce compact preview advice. Final execution independently admits current filesystem state, replans the complete input, publishes through compare-and-swap, verifies readback, and settles durable facts as defined in Section 2. Preview advice has no authority over that result and is replaced by the terminal tool outcome.
+
+Runtime disposal first stops admission, clears retained input state, awaits any in-progress lazy worker creation, and then awaits worker termination. Disposal cannot leave worker startup or worker lifetime outside the runtime generation that owns it.
+
+### 16. Attribution Never Owns Tool Outcomes
+
+Direct file tools accumulate only mutations whose durable publication settled. Their exactly-once terminal boundary offers one immutable attribution job without awaiting it; zero-change calls offer nothing, and partial publication offers only the settled prefix. Attribution state is operation-scoped memory, not a durable fact or tool-result field.
+
+A Bash command declared with `mutates` publishes through the same file edit publisher, and its landing, not the tool call, offers the settled files, because a managed command lands after its call settled. It offers the same one direct job for its settled prefix and never waits for it. A command without `mutates` adds no attribution launch or wait.
+
