+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- A new tool cannot inherit an accidental concurrency policy.
+- Unsafe effects cannot overlap other calls in the same attempt.
+- Bounded tools cannot hang a turn indefinitely.
+- Execution mode does not create a policy bypass.
+- The product states honestly where containment lives.
+- Ordinary commands remain owned while they stay in their platform container; hostile escape containment belongs to an external sandbox.
+- One ordinary settlement order serves every caller, so a result is never published while its owned platform container is still running.
+- A guardian's death costs the work that guardian held and nothing more: the host starts a new guardian at its next launch, so tools and executors launch again with no restart of the account service.
+
+### Negative
+
+- One slow unsafe call delays everything queued behind it.
+- Crash recovery retains any operation whose precondition no longer holds and any absent target whose publication could have started parent creation, because it has no publication-specific actor proof. The cell stays retained across starts, with one warning receipt per cell generation and later reports at DEBUG.
+- A concurrency declaration is a semantic promise the compiler cannot prove.
+- A wrongly owned watchdog exemption can still produce a stuck resource.
+- Flywheel is unsuitable where in-application approval is mandatory.
+- A launch costs a supervisor process per command and one guardian handshake before the payload runs.
+- A guardian whose control channel fails while it lives is ended with the work it holds, because only its proven exit may end that work and its failed channel cannot reach it.
+- Windows containment is weaker than Unix containment, and the product says so instead of hiding the gap.
+- Persistent work is intentionally unavailable after host release, including its retained output and cleanup controls.
+
+### Risks
+
+- A mutating tool is incorrectly marked safe.
+- A new dispatch route skips the shared gate.
+- `unboundedDuration` is used without another tested lifecycle owner.
+- Users mistake auditability, command redirection, or the read-only rule for containment.
+- An agent changes project files with plain shell writes, so those changes are absent from edit cards, session change review, and attribution.
+- A new module reaches a platform launch directly and leaves the owned boundary.
+- A caller publishes a result before its owned platform container settles, and races the guardian's kill list or platform tree termination.
+- Process-group containment is mistaken for hostile escape containment without an external sandbox.
+- On macOS a leaderless process group is read as the recorded container. While no guardian runs, the recorded group can empty, and an unrelated process can take its pid, lead a new group, and exit while that group's members still run. The next guardian's recovery then ends that group. macOS withholds the environment of its platform programs, such as `/bin/bash`, so the container mark cannot prove membership there. On Linux the same case remains only for a group with a member whose environment the kernel withholds, such as a non-dumpable or set-user-ID program.
+- On Linux, a leaderless group of the launch whose remaining members all cleared or rewrote their environment, such as `env -i` or a process title that overwrites the environment area, carries no mark, so recovery ends only the launch's marked processes outside that group and leaves the group running.
+- When a recorded container's pid names another process or another container at the next start, recovery drops the record with no signal, so the launch's marked processes outside the group keep running. This needs a hard kill, a detached process of the launch, an emptied group, and a reuse of the pid before the next start.
+- A persistent launch is exposed without effective host authorization and per-call opt-in.
+
+---
+
+## Rejected Alternatives
+
+### Default Concurrency Classification
+
+Rejected because either default silently grants a semantic property no tool author chose.
+
+### Only Per-File Serialization
+
+Rejected because arbitrary shell, network, and dynamic effects are not reducible to known file paths.
+
+### Serialize Every Tool
+
+Rejected because independent reads, searches, and subagents benefit materially from bounded parallel execution.
+
+### General Pre-Invocation Approval
+
+Rejected because it conflicts with hands-off operation and does not provide containment against arbitrary process capabilities. The post-safety module gate is narrower: it can change one already-admitted execution but cannot grant authority, bypass safety admission, or replace the execution owner.
+
+### Hardcoded Command Deny-List
+
+Rejected because shell can route around patterns, creating the appearance of security without a boundary. Static write-target analysis instead decides on the resolved targets of recognized effects. It is a guard against mistakes, not a security boundary.
+
+### Implicit Detachment Or Reparenting
+
+Rejected as a persistence contract. Shell syntax, `setsid`, and a double fork leave the process group but keep the container mark, so the command's stop still ends them. Registration with `launchd` or `systemd` leaves Flywheel's boundary altogether. Neither is managed persistent work. Persistent survival is a separate, explicit capability with host policy, per-call opt-in, retained output, live management, and a final release boundary. Deployments that must prevent hostile escape use an external sandbox.
+
+### Waiting For Running Commands At A Replacement
+
+Rejected because one long build or dev server in one project would keep the whole account, every project and every client, on the previous build for as long as the command runs, with no deadline. A command needs neither its executor's pipes nor the project store: its output is a log file and its exit status belongs to its own supervisor. Handing the running command over therefore costs one old-build process per command, while keeping a project on the old build would split one project store between two owners.
+
+### Killing By Root Pid Alone
+
+Rejected because a payload's exit is not its process container's end and a pid is recycled. On macOS and Linux, the process group is the unit a decision addresses and group emptiness is what ends ownership; the supervisor sends TERM to its own unchanged process group and KILL to its proven members, without a separate identity check before each signal. A stop or an end adds each marked process outside the group, and that process is signalled only while its start stamp is proven the same as when it was first seen. On Windows, a job object is the unit a decision addresses, its kernel-reported membership is what ends ownership, and the container's name is unique per launch; a persistent process, which cannot be held by a handle, uses pid plus creation time and a bounded tree walk instead.
+
+### The Writer Removes Its Own Spool
+
+Rejected because a writer cannot see its own end. A runtime that is killed, or a payload that crashes, never runs its cleanup, and `flywheel kill` and a service manager's exit timeout can both end a runtime with SIGKILL. A writer also does not know that its stdout is a spool: the same runtime starts under launchd, systemd, or Task Scheduler with other streams. The command supervisor outlives its payload, but it exists only for supervised launches and dies with the same signals. The kernel still answers for a process after it is gone, so the process that reads the kernel afterward, the launcher or the next launch, is the owner that sees every end.
+
+---
+
